const ACRONYMS_HTML=`<h3 id="lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</h3><dl>
<dt>2D / 3D</dt><dd>Two-dimensional / three-dimensional geometry: a planar domain / geometry with elevation as well as horizontal position.</dd>
<dt>pp</dt><dd>Percentage points: the arithmetic difference between two percentages, rather than a percentage change relative to one of them.</dd>
<dt>CPU / GPU</dt><dd>Central processing unit / graphics processing unit. The visibility solvers and benchmark here run as JavaScript on the central processor; browser display is a separate operation.</dd>
<dt>CGAL</dt><dd>Computational Geometry Algorithms Library, a separate library cited for published algorithms and complexity bounds. This page does not run that library.</dd>
<dt>BVH</dt><dd>Bounding volume hierarchy: a tree of enclosing boxes used to reject groups of triangles or edges before testing them individually.</dd>
<dt>LOS</dt><dd>Line of sight: the straight segment from the observer to a target. Visibility requires no intervening opaque geometry.</dd>
<dt>TIN / DEM</dt><dd>Triangulated irregular network / digital elevation model. Here terrain is a network of planar triangles. A raster elevation model samples a grid and needs an interpolation rule between samples.</dd>
<dt>GRASS</dt><dd>Geographic Resources Analysis Support System, the geographic software whose terrain-grid viewshed documentation is cited below.</dd>
<dt>JIT</dt><dd>Just-in-time compilation: a browser can compile frequently executed JavaScript while the page runs, changing timing after warmup.</dd>
<dt>PNG / JSON</dt><dd>Portable Network Graphics / JavaScript Object Notation: the raster image export format / the text format for saved settings and benchmark results.</dd>
<dt>SVG / HTML</dt><dd>Scalable Vector Graphics / HyperText Markup Language: editable vector diagram elements / the page's controls and selectable explanations.</dd>
<dt>IEEE 754</dt><dd>The floating-point standard of the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers. <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/ecmascript-data-types-and-values.html#sec-ecmascript-language-types-number-type" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">JavaScript numbers use its binary64 format</a>; rounding errors remain, and tolerances are not a proof of geometric robustness.</dd>
<dt>SIGGRAPH / JASA / SDH</dt><dd>Special Interest Group on Computer Graphics and Interactive Techniques / Journal of the Acoustical Society of America / Spatial Data Handling. These abbreviations identify conferences or publications in the sources.</dd>
<dt>ID</dt><dd>Identifier: an integer linking a result fragment or camera cell to its original receiver triangle. It records ownership rather than a geometric quantity.</dd>
</dl>`;
const SOURCES_HTML=`<p><b>Continuous 2D visibility:</b> Bungiu, Hemmer, Hershberger, Huang &amp; Kröller (2014), <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.3905" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efficient Computation of Visibility Polygons</a>; the <a href="https://doc.cgal.org/latest/Visibility_2/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Computational Geometry Algorithms Library (CGAL) visibility manual</a>. The lab implements its own opening-based propagation, not a binding to that library. The <a href="https://doc.cgal.org/latest/Triangulation_2/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">triangulation manual</a> describes other point-location strategies, including walks and triangulation hierarchies; the lab uses a bounding-box tree.</p>
<p><b>Shadow boundaries:</b> McGuire, <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/gpugems/gpugems/part-ii-lighting-and-shadows/chapter-9-efficient-shadow-volume-rendering" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efficient Shadow Volume Rendering</a>; Stich, Wächter &amp; Keller, <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/gpugems/gpugems3/part-ii-light-and-shadows/chapter-11-efficient-and-robust-shadow-volumes-using" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efficient and Robust Shadow Volumes</a>. These discuss silhouette extrusion and stencil-based rendering. Here the central processing unit (CPU) clips receiver polygons directly; no stencil counts or graphics processing unit (GPU) timing supplies the answer.</p>
<p><b>Convex-cell beams:</b> Funkhouser et al., <a href="https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~funk/sig98.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">A Beam Tracing Approach to Acoustic Modeling for Interactive Virtual Environments</a>, 1998, and <a href="https://gfx.cs.princeton.edu/gfx/pubs/Funkhouser_2004_ABT/index.php" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">A Beam Tracing Method for Interactive Architectural Acoustics</a>, 2004. The first is a computer-graphics conference paper; the second appears in the Journal of the Acoustical Society of America. They provide the general cell/beam principle. This lab independently adapts it to direct visibility above a terrain mesh, without acoustic reflections or diffraction.</p>
<p><b>Terrain and projected surface context:</b> De Floriani &amp; Magillo, <a href="https://www.geos.ed.ac.uk/~gisteac/gis_book_abridged/files/ch38.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Intervisibility on terrains</a>; Suleiman, Joliveau &amp; Favier, <i>A New Algorithm for 3D Isovist</i> (2012), supplied Spatial Data Handling conference manuscript, <i>SDH2012.pdf</i>, pages 9–12; Chang &amp; Park (2011), <a href="https://doi.org/10.5659/AIKAR.2011.13.3.19" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Exploration of Isovist Fields to Model 3D Visibility With Building Facade</a>. These are conceptual context; the lab's clipped terrain fragments and camera transformation are its own implementation.</p>
<p><b>Ray acceleration and raster terrain:</b> <a href="https://pbr-book.org/4ed/Primitives_and_Intersection_Acceleration/Bounding_Volume_Hierarchies" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Physically Based Rendering: Bounding Volume Hierarchies</a> explains group rejection during ray traversal. <a href="https://grass.osgeo.org/grass-stable/manuals/r.viewshed.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Geographic Resources Analysis Support System (GRASS) r.viewshed</a> documents a terrain-grid sweep with bilinear elevation interpolation. Its terrain and output model differ from this lab's six-face camera-depth rasterizer, planar occupancy-grid traversal, and separate vector-wall cell-mask benchmark.</p>
<p><b>Isovist measures:</b> Hosseini Alamdari et al. (2022), <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/23998083221083680" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">New algorithms for generating isovist field and isovist measurements</a>; Pech, Lam &amp; Masek (2020), <a href="https://ro.ecu.edu.au/ecuworkspost2013/9356/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Quantifiable Isovist and Graph-Based Measures for Automatic Evaluation of Different Area Types in Virtual Terrain Generation</a>. Visible volume, projected occupation and physical visible surface area are distinct measures. The patch and camera-cell surface estimators illustrated here are lab implementations.</p>`;
const COST_HTML=`<p><b>Compare the same output.</b> A ray–triangle intersection can locate a geometric hit accurately while the region between samples remains approximate. Input detail, floating-point tolerances and the chosen reconstruction affect every method. Compare time and spatial error for the same observer, geometry and deliverable.</p>
<p>In the table, <b>n</b> is the number of 2D boundary edges or vertices, <b>h</b> is holes + 1, <b>t</b> is terrain triangles, <b>R</b> is tested directions or targets, <b>P</b> is raster cells, and <b>K</b> is beam entries or fragments actually processed. Published bounds refer to the named algorithm, not automatically to this teaching implementation. <a href="#lab-glossary">Abbreviations are explained below.</a></p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Work and reuse</th><th>Useful output / environment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>2D boundary scan</td><td>Joe–Simpson: O(n) query for a simple polygon without holes.</td><td>A single visibility polygon in a simple floor plan.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2D rotational sweep</td><td>Asano: O(n log n) query for polygons with holes. This lab's independent event-sector reference instead scans n walls for each of O(n) intervals, giving O(n²).</td><td>General polygonal visibility without a reusable triangulation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2D triangular expansion</td><td>The Computational Geometry Algorithms Library reports O(nh) query after triangulation; its manual lists O(n) preprocessing. Locate the observer, then follow only surviving openings. The lab uses embedded triangulations and a separate point-location tree.</td><td>Repeated continuous isovists in static corridors and urban footprints; walls can terminate whole branches.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Finite rays or targets</td><td>Brute force O(Rn) for segments or O(Rt) for triangles. A bounding volume hierarchy rejects groups; worst-case traversal can still test all primitives. Reuse the index across observer changes.</td><td>Selected line-of-sight queries, large meshes, or sampled image output. Sampling resolution remains a separate choice from intersection acceleration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3D air-cell beams</td><td>Preprocess a conforming volume mesh. Work follows K entries and their aperture/plane complexity; repeated entries can multiply.</td><td>Continuous boundary pieces in compartmentalized space. Open or finely partitioned landscapes may keep many beams alive.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Geometric shadow subtraction</td><td>Initial all-pairs terrain work is O(t²), followed by fragment-dependent clipping. Conservative half-space rejection avoids many full clips; splitting increases storage and later work.</td><td>Small meshes requiring continuous triangle-interior cuts and surface measures. Production scale needs stronger spatial/angular culling and robust geometry.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Camera depth raster</td><td>Project and clip triangles, test covered cell centers and retain nearest depth. Work depends on projected triangles, candidate pixels, overlapping fragments and clearing P entries.</td><td>Dense camera visibility or shadow images. Physical surface area additionally needs back-projection onto the original triangles.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Terrain-grid viewshed</td><td>For a square elevation grid, naive line-of-sight walks can cost O(P√P). GRASS r.viewshed documents an O(P log P) sweep that reuses nearby queries.</td><td>Large digital elevation models (DEMs) when a terrain-grid mask is wanted. This is a different algorithm from camera rasterization.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><b>Geometry changes the tradeoff.</b> Broad walls and ridges can reject many directions at once. Narrow gaps and frequent depth changes require many samples or many geometric fragments. A triangulated irregular network (TIN), a bilinearly interpolated elevation grid, and voxels represent different surfaces; a solver cannot recover detail missing from its input. Terrain-only height fields do not represent overhangs and façades.</p>
<p><b>Sampling scale:</b> a small feature of width w at distance d subtends roughly w/d radians. Uniform angular spacing should be appreciably smaller; covering two angular dimensions can require sample counts proportional to (d/w)². This is geometric intuition, not a guaranteed error bound. Cube-map cells are uniform in camera coordinates, not equal in solid angle. Halving raster cell width quadruples cell count for a fixed 2D domain.</p>
<p><b>Local timing:</b> “Effort and raster” times fresh central-processor JavaScript calls after warmup and reports surface reconstruction separately. Drawing and mismatch evaluation are excluded. Just-in-time compilation (JIT), allocations, scheduling, hierarchy quality and graphics-processor acceleration can change rankings. Three measured calls provide a local median, not a confidence interval or production benchmark.</p>`;
const DOCS={
shadows:{title:'Construct shadow boundaries or subtract them from receivers',method:`<div class="cols"><div><h3>Construct the boundary</h3><p>For each closed box, compare its face normals with the direction toward the source. An edge joining a source-facing face to a source-away face is a <b>silhouette edge</b>. Extending its endpoints away from the source makes a shadow-volume side. Caps are needed to close that volume.</p><p>This mode explains the boundary, then paints the resulting shadows on the ground, roofs and walls. The purple source can be dragged in either view. The linked plan projects the same highlighted geometry from above; vertical faces can collapse to edges.</p></div><div><h3>Subtract shadows, facet by facet</h3><p>The second mode exposes the receiver calculation. A triangulated building facet is the current occluder. Inspect it, extend its shadow, intersect that shadow with the current candidate receiver polygons, then retain the pieces outside it.</p><p>Repeat for every building facet. Teal candidates shrink as more shadows are removed; shaded pieces have no direct connection to the source. Mutual building obstruction and self-occluded faces are included. Turn off <b>Show construction</b> for the final shaded scene.</p></div></div>`,detail:`<h3>Input, classification and extrusion</h3><p>The input consists of closed axis-aligned boxes, triangulated ground, roof and wall receivers, and movable point q. Each box face has outward unit normal n. The sign of n·(q−a), for a face vertex a, classifies it as source-facing or source-away. Edges shared by opposite classifications form the silhouette. This classification alone is not the final shadow test: another building can block a source-facing receiver.</p><p>For silhouette endpoint a, the drawn extension is a + λ(a−q). The endpoint pair and its extensions form a quadrilateral shadow side. These drawn sides are cropped to the display domain. Their finite length does not determine visibility. The silhouette mode illustrates the boundary of the closed object; receiver-subtraction modes instead show continuous source-to-facet construction rays back to q.</p><h3>Actual receiver subtraction</h3><p>Each building face is split into triangular occluders and receivers. For occluder a,b,c, three planes through q and its edges enclose an angular cone; the facet's supporting plane starts the shadow behind that facet. A plane is represented by normal n and offset d; its retained side satisfies n·p−d ≥ 0. Side normals come from (a−q)×(b−q), with signs chosen toward the cone interior. Orient the base plane so q is outside. These four half-spaces define a depth-aware convex shadow.</p><p>Start each receiver at its whole triangle. If all of its vertices lie outside one shadow half-space, it has no positive-area overlap and is retained. Otherwise clip to find the overlap. An edge crossing a plane creates p=a + f(a)/(f(a)−f(b))·(b−a), where f(p)=n·p−d. For an actual overlap, walk through the shadow planes: emit the outside polygon, carry the inside remainder to the next plane, and discard the final inside polygon. Emitted polygons retain their original receiver identifiers. Subsequent occluders process these surviving fragments.</p><p>The facet-by-facet mode records preparation, actual overlap and partition checkpoints. A facet whose shadow misses all current candidates makes no visible cut. The operation readouts count the corresponding signed tests and polygon clips across all receivers; extrusion drawing and screen projection are excluded. Repeated fragments can make the work much larger than the original face count. The original bounded receiver model also includes artificial side and ceiling boundary triangles; their subtraction work remains in the counters, although physical surface readouts include only ground, roofs and walls. This teaching implementation does not use a spatial hierarchy to remove all distant receiver–occluder pairs.</p><h3>Display, depth and precision</h3><p>Coplanar fragments are grouped by complete receiver face for display. Shared subdivision seams cancel before visible/hidden boundaries are outlined. Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) display occlusion removes screen regions blocked by nearer opaque faces; those drawing operations never enter receiver surface area or solver counters. Orthographic depth is affine over a planar face, allowing overlap to be split where planes cross. Transparent construction layers retain centroid-depth ordering and can have approximate ordering at intersections.</p><p>Calculations use binary64 floating-point numbers. Coplanar contact with the occluder's own base plane is regularized; positive-area slivers below 10⁻⁸ are removed. Normal construction uses the whole polygon to handle collinear leading vertices. Turning construction off changes presentation, not the solved fragments. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
expansion:{title:'Point location → shared opening → clipped triangle',method:`<h3>How the first triangle is found</h3><p>A triangle contains the observer when the observer is on its interior side for all three directed edges. These are orientation tests. The default <b>bounding-box index</b> first rejects groups of triangles whose axis-aligned bounds do not contain the point, then applies those three tests to the candidates. “Find the observer triangle” animates that search. Select a linear scan for comparison. The box index is a teaching implementation; the Computational Geometry Algorithms Library (CGAL) also offers walking and triangulation-hierarchy strategies.</p><h3>What moves during expansion?</h3><p>The observer stays fixed. Each gray mesh edge has a stored neighboring-triangle identifier. A shared free-space edge is called a <b>portal</b>, meaning an artificial opening introduced by triangulation. Following that identifier advances the calculation into the adjacent triangle.</p><p>The incoming straight-line fan clips the shared edge. Purple is the whole edge, orange is its surviving part. Extend the two orange endpoint directions <b>past the opening</b>: they bound the fan inside the next triangle. Keep the portion between them. Dark teal lines persist where a cut crosses the interior of an original triangle. A wall has no neighbor and stops that branch.</p><p>Different branches around holes may reach one triangle through disjoint openings. An unrestricted flood fill would instead reach around corners, which is not visibility.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Mesh and point-location state</h3><p>The input is a polygonal free-space domain with obstacle holes and a constrained triangulation. Every triangle is oriented counterclockwise. An edge-to-triangle map pairs matching endpoints and stores each neighbor; an unpaired edge is a domain or obstacle boundary. For directed edge a→b, the orientation value is (b−a)×(q−a). All three values at least −10⁻⁸ identify a containing triangle.</p><p>The bounding volume hierarchy (BVH) encloses triangle groups in axis-aligned boxes. It splits by the widest box axis and median primitive-center coordinate, down to at most four triangles per leaf. A point query rejects boxes not containing q, then performs triangle side tests in surviving leaves. Overlapping boxes mean several branches can survive. A linear query tests every triangle.</p><p>“Find the observer triangle” shows the recorded search only through its first successful triangle and holds there at completion. That is the displayed explanation, not an early-exit optimization of the computational core. The core gathers all incident containing triangles, needed when q lies on an artificial shared edge. Those roots seed the subsequent expansion. Obstacles are excluded by the observer-validity margin of 0.02 scene units.</p><h3>Opening propagation</h3><p>A queue item holds triangle, previous triangle, parent entry, depth, shared portal, surviving opening and inward half-planes through q. Clip the whole portal segment against the incoming half-planes. Where endpoint signed values f(a) and f(b) change sign, the intersection is a + f(a)/(f(a)−f(b))·(b−a). The two surviving endpoint directions define the next straight-line cone; clip the neighboring triangle against it.</p><p>The observer never moves with the queue. An edge behind the observer relative to the current triangle, the incoming edge, and seeded roots are not followed again. Walls end propagation. Distinct openings can enter one triangle separately, so a single global visited flag would lose visibility. Result fragments retain their parent chain and sum to visible area; cuts do not alter the input mesh. Segments shorter than 10⁻⁸ and fragments below 10⁻⁸ area are dropped, and excessive queue growth raises an error. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
air:{title:'A 3D opening is a shared face between air cells',method:`<p>The triangulated irregular network (TIN) partitions the ground surface. To extend the free-space idea from 2D, partition the <b>air above it</b> into tetrahedra. Each triangular ground prism is divided consistently into three tetrahedra. Adjoining faces are paired during preprocessing.</p><p>Locate the observer's air cell by testing its face half-spaces. A whole shared face is purple. Intersect it with the incoming polygonal beam: the surviving opening is orange. It may be a triangle or a more complex convex polygon. The beam's side planes pass through the original observer and the opening edges.</p><p>Clip the next air cell by those planes. The resulting green polyhedron shows the reachable portion. Its new cut edges are explicitly outlined. Each outgoing shared face repeats the operation. A ground face terminates the beam and contributes a visible terrain polygon. Completed ground cuts remain visible as the process advances.</p><p>“Follow one beam” exposes one path through this branching calculation. “Accumulate all terrain beams” accumulates every branch and finishes with the full visible surface. The projected-cut mode uses this same main view and its insets to connect a selected physical terrain triangle with its surviving observer-facing footprint. Single-click a terrain triangle to select it; the selection persists in the other terrain tabs. Repeated visits to an air cell are counted separately from unique cells. The observer-detail inset centers on the current whole shared face, surviving opening or clipped air cell, depending on the stage, while always fitting the entire input face. It projects the same green filled terrain and cell edges highlighted in the main view and plan. The input face and its vertex letters are inspection overlays drawn above the terrain, so an obstruction cannot hide the construction being explained. Matching letters identify original and newly cut vertices in the main view and observer inset. Its plan inset projects the same cells and limiting rays vertically. Scroll the main view to zoom; wireframe opacity can be adjusted separately from showing the complete input mesh.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Build the air-cell graph</h3><p>The synthetic triangulated irregular network (TIN) has a single height at each horizontal position. Terrain density selects 6×4, 10×7 or 12×8 grid cells, each split into two planar terrain triangles. Each terrain triangle is extended vertically to a planar ceiling, and its prism is divided consistently into three tetrahedra. Vertex ordering gives matching subdivisions on adjacent prism faces. Matching triangular faces establish cell adjacency.</p><p>The ceiling is max(highest terrain vertex, q elevation) + max(0.25, selected clearance). A straight segment between q and any terrain point cannot rise above both endpoints, so this cap preserves all direct terrain sightlines. Clearance prevents degenerate tetrahedra at peaks; it is an analysis boundary, not a sky model. Moving q horizontally recomputes its interpolated ground height plus the selected eye height.</p><h3>Beam state and transmission</h3><p>Containment tests each tetrahedron's four outward half-spaces n·q−d ≤ 10⁻⁸; all incident containing cells are seeded. A queue entry stores cell, incoming face, parent, depth, aperture and beam side planes. Clip an outgoing shared face against the incoming beam. For aperture vertices aᵢ, side normals come from (aᵢ−q)×(aᵢ₊₁−q), oriented toward the aperture interior. Every plane still passes through q.</p><p>A surviving internal aperture sends its cone to the neighbor. A terrain boundary instead records a visible receiver polygon; a ceiling or side boundary stops at the finite domain. The clipped next-cell illustration also creates cap polygons where beam planes cut tetrahedron edges. These caps describe the current beam inside air, not new terrain faces.</p><h3>Observer inset and vertex correspondence</h3><p>The observer-detail camera stays at q and aims at the current face, opening or clipped-cell centroid. Its rectangular window fits that stage's geometry together with the entire input face, with extra room for labels, while keeping the stage centroid centered. If this direction puts an input corner behind the camera, use the observer-facing face normal instead. Project points by dividing their camera-right and camera-up coordinates by positive forward depth. Polygon and segment drawing clips at depth 0.01 and at the inset rectangle.</p><p>The green polygon fills and line segments come from the same current-stage scene as the external view and plan. The completed viewshed is not substituted for the current branch. Draw the complete input-face outline and surviving opening above terrain context as inspection overlays; this deliberate display order keeps the construction readable even when terrain would hide part of it. A shared list labels the original face vertices A, B and C, then assigns further letters to distinct new opening or target-cut vertices. Both views use those same physical points and letters, with a white text outline for readability. These camera and layering choices do not change the visibility calculation.</p><h3>Output and limits</h3><p>Output includes parent-linked beam entries and terminal polygons. Physical terrain area sums only terminal terrain fragments; boundary caps are excluded. Multiple entries can reach a cell, and each is kept. Tiny area and solid-angle apertures are rejected; growth beyond the 80,000-entry guard reports failure. Wireframes expose input cells, while completed cut edges expose query fragments. Reflections, diffraction and unrestricted traversal around corners are absent. A real urban model would need a volume decomposition conforming to buildings and overhangs, not just these vertical terrain prisms. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
terrain:{title:'Subtract depth-aware terrain shadows from candidate surfaces',method:`<p>Choose an opaque terrain facet. Three planes through its edges and the observer define its angular shadow; a fourth plane starts that shadow at the facet. This depth condition preserves receiver geometry in front of the obstruction.</p><p><b>Only the highlighted face's shadow</b> clips every terrain receiver against that one shadow. After the extrusion, it follows real receiver-by-receiver classification, overlap and partition checkpoints. Its final teal remainder is a candidate surface: other possible occluders are deliberately ignored.</p><p><b>Subtract shadows, face by face</b> repeats a four-stage sequence for every terrain occluder: inspect the facet, construct its shadow, show actual receiver overlaps, then keep the remainder. Accepted teal candidates shrink as further shadows are removed.</p><p>Nearby-first and spiral change processing order. Each mode preserves depth through the facet base plane. Full accumulation produces the continuous terrain viewshed; the shaded and retained polygons cover physical sloping surfaces.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Define and subtract one shadow</h3><p>For opaque facet a,b,c and observer q, each edge defines an observer-through-edge side plane. Its normal is proportional to (a−q)×(b−q); orient it toward the facet's cone interior. Add the facet's base plane, oriented so q is outside and geometry behind the facet is inside. The shadow is the intersection of these four positive half-spaces n·p−d ≥ 0. Omitting the base plane would incorrectly remove foreground receivers whose projected directions merely overlap.</p><p>For every current receiver fragment, reject the shadow when all vertices lie outside any one half-space. Otherwise clip against the planes to find actual overlap. During subtraction, emit the outside portion of the remaining polygon at each plane and continue with its inside portion. Discard the final inside remainder. The emitted polygons are disjoint except along shared boundaries. Edge crossings create interpolated vertices, so the method can cut a triangle's interior even when its original vertices alone do not reveal the obstruction.</p><h3>Selected shadow and full accumulation</h3><p>One-face mode keeps only the selected occluder's operation against every receiver. A single click chooses the occluder, with that selection shared by the other terrain tabs. Automatic selection searches the near ridge for an illustrative blocking face. The single-shadow timeline reserves 5% for choosing the facet, 5% for preparing planes, 25% for continuously growing the extrusion, 50% for the recorded receiver checkpoints and 15% for the completed result. During extrusion growth the work count stays at its preparation checkpoint. Previous / Next step follows the exact start of each stage or receiver checkpoint. After constructing the shadow, the animation highlights the current receiver and follows its recorded classification, actual overlap, and retained partition. A receiver that misses the shadow still contributes its real rejection work. The teal remainder is not yet a solution accounting for every obstruction.</p><p>The accumulated mode initializes every receiver with its whole terrain triangle and subtracts every facet's shadow. It retains preparation, overlap and partition checkpoints for each occluder, using the same shadow-subtraction kernel as the selected-face mode. Dynamic counters report only work reached at the displayed checkpoint, including rejected fragment candidates. Polygon clips advance when actual tests and partitions occur; explanatory extrusion length does not invent fractional work.</p><p>Nearby-first sorts centroids by three-dimensional distance. Spiral sorts horizontal distance into outward bands and then azimuth. Neither is a general depth order. Both are valid subtraction orders because every shadow includes its base plane and every occluder is processed. In real arithmetic they remove the same union of shadows; floating-point ordering can leave small differences.</p><h3>Relation to the observer viewport</h3><p>This tab is <b>occluder-first</b>: apply one facet's shadow to all current receivers, then move to the next occluder. Terrain in the view is <b>receiver-first</b>: select a receiver clipped to the viewport, apply all occluders to it, then accept its surviving pieces and move to the next receiver. The physical shadow test is the same. The viewport tab restricts its output domain and exposes the work for that smaller domain; it does not infer depth from projected overlap alone.</p><p>All-pairs candidate work is proportional to receiver count times occluder count before fragment growth. Cheap half-space rejection avoids many full clips, but splitting increases subsequent work and storage. Normals use the whole polygon; source-on-plane, tangent and coplanar contacts are regularized. Fragments below 10⁻⁸ area are dropped. This is continuous floating-point geometry, not certified exact arithmetic. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
projection:{title:'Build the viewport from depth-tested receiver fragments',method:`<p>First choose a target receiver. Clip its physical triangle to the observer's viewport and project that candidate. Process its terrain occluders through preparation, actual shadow-overlap detection and fragment partitioning, then accept the surviving green fragments. The final acceptance stage removes orange and purple construction geometry so the piece being added is unambiguous. A fully hidden target contributes nothing.</p><p><b>Explain the projected cut</b> stays with one selected receiver. <b>Accumulate the observer view</b> repeats the same calculation for every triangle intersecting the fixed forward viewport, including candidates that will later prove fully hidden. It processes nearby centroids first and accumulates accepted green surfaces.</p><p>The complete viewport looks along +X, with −Y on its right. In the selected-receiver mode, Target detail optionally aims the observer camera toward that face and fits its extent; turning it off uses the fixed forward viewport. Accumulation always uses the fixed +X viewport and never follows its current target. The framed viewport in the external view identifies the projected copy. Matching letters refer to the same physical vertices in both images. The orbiting illustration camera does not move the observer or change the visibility result.</p><p>This is receiver-first shadow subtraction, using the same depth-aware half-space test as Terrain: shadow cuts. The difference is traversal and output domain. Physical area is measured on surviving sloping terrain in m², not as screen occupation or air volume.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Prepare viewport candidates</h3><p>Let observer q have forward unit vector f, right r and up u. The accumulated view uses f=(1,0,0), r=(0,−1,0), u=(0,0,1). For point p, depth z=(p−q)·f and perspective coordinates are x=(p−q)·r/z, y=(p−q)·u/z. Clip before dividing by z. A near half-space enforces z ≥ 0.01; four side planes enforce xmin·z ≤ (p−q)·r ≤ xmax·z and ymin·z ≤ (p−q)·u ≤ ymax·z. Each boundary is a linear plane through q, except the offset near plane.</p><p>Clip every input receiver triangle against these five planes. Keep positive-area intersections as viewport candidates and sort their centroids by distance to q. This preparation includes triangles later blocked completely: the visibility result is not used to decide whether a candidate enters the traversal. Centroid distance is an explanatory order, not a proof that whole triangles have been depth-sorted. Occluders outside the viewport remain available: their shadow can still reach a candidate inside it.</p><h3>Process one receiver continuously</h3><p>Start from the viewport-clipped polygon on the physical terrain plane. For every terrain occluder, build three angular side planes through q and the occluder's edges, plus the occluder base plane starting its shadow behind that face. A projected overlap alone is insufficient: a foreground receiver sharing the same directions must remain visible.</p><p>Reject a shadow if all candidate-fragment vertices are outside any one half-space. Otherwise clip to obtain the actual overlap. Partition each affected fragment plane by plane, retaining outside pieces and discarding the final inside remainder. Candidate blockers under test are highlighted, including those in rejection batches. Construction rays continue back to q rather than beginning at an artificial near-plane point. Changed blockers have four explanatory stages: prepare the shadow, extend it, detect the actual overlap, and retain the partitioned remainder. Preparation and extension share one recorded work checkpoint because extension is display geometry. The trace records signed predicate evaluations and polygon passes at preparation, overlap and partition checkpoints. It exposes candidates, prepared blockers, actual overlapping shadows and retained fragments before accepting the completed target. Consecutive blockers that make no cut are grouped into display steps of at most ⌈N/8⌉ blockers, where N is the terrain occluder count. Every blocker still executes its actual rejection tests; the group holds the final cumulative checkpoint. Repeated receiver processing builds the accumulated viewport without sampling rays or raster cells.</p><p>After all occluders, accept only the surviving green physical fragments and their perspective footprints. Their owner identifiers link them to the original receiver. Hidden targets add zero area. Summing ½‖(pᵢ−p₀)×(pᵢ₊₁−p₀)‖ over polygon triangle fans yields physical area; off-viewport terrain is excluded. Perspective footprint area is in normalized screen units and is neither square meters nor automatically a solid angle.</p><h3>What differs from shadow cuts?</h3><p>Terrain: shadow cuts loops over <b>occluders, then all current receivers</b>. This tab loops over <b>viewport receivers, then all occluders</b>. They subtract the same depth-aware convex shadows with the same half-plane clipping arithmetic. Restrict the full shadow-cut result to the same viewport and their retained surfaces should agree within floating-point tolerance. The receiver traversal changes when results become available and can avoid processing off-viewport receivers; it does not introduce a different physical definition of visibility.</p><p>The lab rebuilds shadow planes during repeated receiver processing and lacks a production spatial/angular hierarchy. Counting signed tests makes the work inspectable but does not capture all plane construction, sorting, allocation or screen drawing costs. Compare complete viewport work with complete scene work only when that scope difference is intentional; use matched-output timing for performance claims.</p><h3>Inspection camera and correspondence</h3><p>The selected-target inspection camera aims f=normalize(c−q) at the target centroid c. If a corner would lie behind that forward plane, use the observer-facing face normal. With world up w=(0,0,1), r=normalize(f×w) and u=r×f; near-vertical gaze uses w=(0,1,0). Fit the entire target in the detail window; blockers and extrusion geometry can extend outside that window. This framing preserves left/right correspondence and does not change q. Turning Target detail off uses the fixed forward domain, which can exclude all or part of the selected face. Accumulation always uses a fixed forward viewport for its calculation and final image.</p><p>A projected point on the physical viewport at depth s is q+s/z·(p−q). Its rectangular frame surrounds the projected copy in the external illustration. Actual cut endpoints use shared letter labels. Coplanar fragment seams cancel before drawing cut edges; purple directions identify nearer blockers, and teal continuations reach their cut vertices. Acceptance removes these construction overlays and keeps only the green target contribution. The beam tab's projected-cut explanation uses its own beam result and omits this external projected copy.</p><p>All terrain backdrop triangles are neutral gray context, including surfaces not yet accepted. A completed visibility solution is not substituted for this backdrop. Near-plane and grazing geometry need tolerance handling; binary64 arithmetic is not exact, and tangential zero-area pieces are omitted. Display occlusion and inspection overlays are separate from solver visibility. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
metrics:{title:'Compare geometric regions with ray and raster samples',method:`<h3>One method and one resolution slider</h3><p>Choose <b>Raycast</b> or <b>Raster</b> above the demonstration. The progress slider controls its resolution directly. All computed sample directions or raster cells are displayed; there is no separate sample-count limit or drawing cap in this tab.</p><h3>2D planar area</h3><p>Teal is the continuous triangle-expansion region, with an independent corner-event boundary. Raycast uses 8 to 1,024 equally spaced directions and joins their first-hit endpoints into the orange polygon. Its shoelace area measures that displayed polygon, not a mean radial distance.</p><p>Raster uses 8 to 128 world-grid columns and about 0.65 times as many rows. It first marks building occupancy at cell centers, then traverses the grid to classify visibility from the observer. Orange is the union of the visible query cells. This is a grid-quantized obstacle and output model, not a vector wall intersection repeated at every cell center. Thin walls or gaps can disappear or expand under that quantization.</p><p>Compare both total area and mismatched area. Missing and extra regions can cancel in the total; mismatch measures their physical symmetric difference. A raster cell and an angular interval represent different spatial resolutions even when their sample counts happen to match.</p><p>For Raycast, <b>Walls versus open boundary</b> instead compares first-hit building-wall lengths, analysis-border lengths and escaped directions. This option applies to angular rays; Raster compares cell area. The border is an artificial planar limit rather than physical sky.</p><h3>3D physical surface area</h3><p>Both methods use six 90° camera faces with N×N cells each. The slider sets N from 2 to 32, giving 6N² = 24 to 6,144 angular samples. Raycast tests the center direction of every cell against terrain triangles. Raster independently projects those triangles and retains nearest depth at the same cell centers; it does not obtain visibility from rays.</p><p>Recover physical area by clipping each winning terrain triangle to the angular cell's four side planes. Orange is the reconstructed sloping terrain footprint. Sky cells contribute no terrain area. Raycast draws every direction, ending misses at the enclosing display box rather than at an arbitrary distance. Raster shows every query pixel in its compact six-face grid and draws no pretend query rays. Teal is the continuous visible-surface reference, excluding sky and artificial boundaries.</p><p>Use overlay or side-by-side views to compare the same scene and observer. Hidden-surface mode compares the physical terrain complements. Optional sampled-cell edges expose reconstruction; shadow envelopes extend reconstructed boundaries for explanation but add no measured surface or volume. Geometric query work and sample query/reconstruction work have separate readouts. An intersection attempt, a depth test and an occupancy-cell visit have different costs. These workload counts explain what was done; they are not elapsed time or a universal performance ranking.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Planar ray reconstruction</h3><p>Let G be the continuous visible region and S the displayed sampled region. Triangle expansion gives G; an independent corner-event reference supplies its boundary. Uniform 2D directions use the centers of equal angular intervals. Intersect each with the nearest boundary edge and connect consecutive endpoints. S has shoelace area ½|Σ(xᵢyᵢ₊₁−yᵢxᵢ₊₁)|. Hits are geometric intersections, but the connections between samples introduce error.</p><p>Clip the sampled observer-centered triangle fans against continuous fragments to find |G∩S|. Missed area is |G|−|G∩S|; extra area is |S|−|G∩S|; mismatch is their sum, |G|+|S|−2|G∩S|. Equal areas can conceal a large mismatch. The enclosing analysis border is a planar limit, not physical sky.</p><p>Raycast wall coverage assigns each angular sample interval to its first-hit edge, clips that edge to the interval and sums the resulting physical wall and border lengths. Escaped-direction percentage counts sample directions reaching the enclosing border. These angular assignments remain approximate between directions and are not the area of a raster mask.</p><h3>Planar occupancy raster</h3><p>For N columns, use round(0.65N) rows over the bounded plan. Classify each grid-cell center as inside a building or free. Only free cells receive visibility queries. Starting at the cell containing the actual observer position, advance through crossed grid boundaries toward each free query-cell center; an occupied or out-of-domain cell blocks the query. When the segment passes exactly through a grid corner, test both side-adjacent cells before moving diagonally, so touching an occupied corner blocks transmission. If quantization marks the observer's own cell occupied, all of these queries are blocked at their start. The obstacles used by this method are these classified cells, rather than the original wall segments. Display every query cell with its visibility classification.</p><p>Sum the physical area of visible cells for S, and intersect their footprints with continuous fragments to measure mismatch. Building-center quantization can miss subcell obstacles, thicken others, alter narrow passages and shift the observer's effective relation to a wall. Increasing N changes both obstacle and output discretization. This independent grid traversal is not the terrain-elevation sweep described by Geographic Resources Analysis Support System (GRASS), and it is not a certified conservative obstacle mask.</p><p>Report occupancy-cell visits separately. They are discrete grid operations, not signed geometric half-plane tests or ray–wall intersection attempts. Grid preparation, query traversal and continuous-reference clipping have distinct roles; avoid treating their raw counts as equally expensive instructions.</p><h3>Matched angular domains in 3D</h3><p>Tile six 90° cube-camera faces with N×N cells each. Forward f, right r and up u form each face's basis. For cell-center coordinates α,β in [−1,1], the direction is f+αr+βu. It is not normalized, so its intersection parameter is forward depth rather than Euclidean range. Both Raycast and Raster use those same 6N² cells.</p><p>Raycast finds the nearest terrain triangle for each direction. Raster clips and projects physical triangles into each face, triangulates clipped footprints, evaluates covered cell centers and interpolates reciprocal depth with screen barycentric weights. The nearest depth supplies the winning triangle identifier (ID). The raster does not run ray–triangle intersections to obtain its winners, nor borrow the reference solution.</p><p>For either method, clip the winning triangle by the angular cell's four bounding planes to recover its physical footprint. Sum ½‖(pᵢ−p₀)×(pᵢ₊₁−p₀)‖ over polygon triangle fans. A sky cell has no receiver and adds no surface. One winner may miss a neighboring triangle or extend into an occluded portion within the cell; cube cells are not equal-solid-angle samples. Reference mismatch clips reconstructed polygons against continuous fragments on their original receiver triangles.</p><h3>Complements and display</h3><p>Hidden surface is the original physical terrain minus the reconstructed visible fragments, excluding artificial sky/domain faces. Sampled-cell wireframes outline those physical footprints. Optional shadow-side envelopes extend their boundaries away from q; these are explanatory extrusions, not a separately validated global volumetric Boolean, and never enter area totals. Overlay and side-by-side use the same observer and corresponding illustration-camera settings.</p><h3>Work, graphics and reuse</h3><p>The 3D query and physical-footprint recovery have separate workload totals. Signed tests, intersection attempts, raster coverage/depth evaluations and grid visits have nonuniform costs. Plane construction, allocation, sorting and display projection are not fully captured by geometric test totals. Use the matched-output timings in Effort and raster to assess elapsed time.</p><p>The progress slider is the sole resolution control in this comparison: 8–1,024 planar rays, 8–128 planar raster columns, or angular N=2–32. For Raycast, every requested direction is drawn. A miss ends where its direction first meets the scene display box, whose coordinates run from 0 to 100 in X, 0 to 70 in Y, and 0 to the current ceiling in Z; this explanatory endpoint adds no terrain area. Raster represents every query pixel in its six grids and does not draw rays for its pixel evaluations. In 2D every occupancy cell is displayed, including occupied and hidden cells, although only free cells have visibility queries. Orbiting the illustration changes presentation but not the observer, sample domain or visibility. Input/observer/resolution caches avoid solving again for an unchanged query. Floating-point clipping and tiny-fragment removal affect both reference and reconstructed areas. The separate Effort and raster tab retains its arbitrary benchmark budgets and wider resolution controls. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
cost:{title:'Measured work, matched output, and a raster baseline',method:`<p>Run the comparison on this browser. Each method gets the same observer and input geometry. The table separates visibility query time from result reconstruction; screen drawing and error evaluation are excluded. Three timed calls follow warmup, and the median is shown. Preparation costs are reported separately.</p><p>In 2D, compare triangular expansion, brute-force rays, indexed rays, and a world-grid mask evaluated at cell centers. The latter is a straightforward <b>raster-output baseline</b> with vector line-of-sight (LOS) tests; it is not an optimized digital-elevation-model (DEM) sweep.</p><p>In 3D, compare continuous shadow clipping, air-cell beams, brute-force angular rays, indexed angular rays, and a real software depth rasterizer. The three angular methods use matching six-face camera sample centers and identical surface reconstruction. A sixth surface-patch result shows the effect of changing the sampling domain.</p><p>The effort counters name their operations: ray/primitive intersections, box tests, beam entries, clipping pairs, or raster fragments. These are not interchangeable unit-cost instructions. Use the measured times and error together.</p><p>The raster-view option exposes the discretization in the same scene. Increase resolution and compare the reconstructed physical area with the continuous reference. A small scalar area error alone does not establish a small boundary error; the benchmark also reports mismatched area where calculated.</p>`,detail:`<h3>What is timed</h3><p>Each timed function is called once for warmup, followed by three independently measured calls; the middle duration is retained together with that call's result. Timings include allocations within the function, exclude SVG drawing and mismatch calculation, and do not include a statistical error estimate. The settings are captured at benchmark start and controls are locked while rows are assembled. Query functions are called directly, bypassing the display-result caches.</p><p>In 2D, the benchmark uses the already embedded triangulation and initialized triangle adjacency. It separately times building the edge bounding volume hierarchy. It does not recreate the original triangulation process or report every previously cached point-location preparation cost. The world-grid mask tests each cell center against all vector edges, costing O(Pn); that is a raster <em>output</em> baseline, not a raster terrain sweep.</p><p>In 3D, triangle indexing and the conforming air-cell mesh/adjacency are prepared separately. Continuous clipping and beams return receiver geometry directly. Angular brute-force rays, indexed rays and software rasterization use identical six-face cell centers. Surface-patch subdivision is a separate preparation cost. Counters count different operations—triangle tests, box tests, fragment/occluder pairs, beam entries, or covered raster fragments—and cannot be compared as identical-cost instructions.</p><h3>Reconstruction and error</h3><p>Angular rays and raster outputs need cell-footprint recovery to obtain physical terrain area. A separately warmed median recovery call is measured for the indexed-ray winners. If raster winners match, that same reconstructed result and recovery timing are reused; differing winners trigger a separate raster recovery and mismatch calculation. Brute-force and indexed ray results are expected to agree and use the same reconstruction. This avoids claiming that projection pixels themselves measure physical area.</p><p>Mismatch rows use symmetric-difference area divided by visible reference area. The beam cross-check reports only absolute difference in total visible area, clearly marked: agreement of that scalar alone does not prove matching boundaries. The chart plots query plus recovery time and mismatch on logarithmic axes; near-zero floors keep values plottable. Tiny timings or errors at those floors should not be overinterpreted.</p><p>These central-processor teaching algorithms are not timings of the Computational Geometry Algorithms Library or graphics hardware. Browser warmup, memory allocation and garbage collection, data layout, robust arithmetic, index quality and GPU parallelism can change the ranking. Compare several observers and geometries at a stated error tolerance before drawing broader conclusions. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
events:{title:'Candidate corners and visible boundaries',method:`<p>A <b>candidate event</b> is an input corner direction. It changes sweep bookkeeping, even when that corner lies behind another wall. A <b>visible-boundary event</b> is a candidate at which the nearest blocking wall changes.</p><p>Dashed continuations connect the nearer wall to the hidden corner that created an otherwise puzzling subdivision. The simpler display groups sectors belonging to the highlighted nearest wall; the calculation retains the candidate intervals. This distinguishes geometric events from uniform directional sampling.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Candidate directions and interval state</h3><p>For every input corner a, compute atan2(aᵧ−qᵧ,aₓ−qₓ), wrap into [0,2π), add both interval endpoints, sort and remove near-duplicates within 10⁻¹¹ radians. In a valid domain with noncrossing boundary edges, the nearest intersected edge cannot change inside an interval that contains no corner direction. A midpoint direction therefore identifies the active blocking edge for that whole interval.</p><p>Intersect the two limiting directions with that same edge to obtain its continuous visible segment. Concatenating the segments gives the polygon boundary. A hidden corner can split an interval without changing its nearest edge: that is a bookkeeping event with no new visible-wall boundary. Candidate-detail mode shows the occluded continuation to that corner; the simpler display groups sectors hitting the highlighted edge. The solver still retains its candidate intervals.</p><h3>Intersection and complexity</h3><p>A ray is q+t d and an edge is a+u(b−a). Solving their 2D cross-product equations gives t and u; retain t ≥ 0 and 0 ≤ u ≤ 1 within tolerance, then choose the smallest t. Nearly parallel pairs are rejected. All n edges are scanned in each of O(n) candidate intervals, so this reference uses O(n²) intersection work plus angle sorting. It does not maintain a balanced active-edge structure like a published O(n log n) rotational sweep.</p><p>This construction supplies an independent boundary/area check for triangle expansion. Zero-width tangential features are regularized, and repeated/collinear directions require tolerances. Unlike uniform ray sampling, representative interval rays derive from geometric corner events; increasing an arbitrary ray count is not how its boundary is refined. The illustration's rotating direction is an explanation cursor, not the observer or an extra physical light source. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`}
};


'use strict';

const WORK_DETAIL_HTML=`<h3>What the operation readouts count</h3><p>“Geometric tests” is a defined workload count, not a count of processor instructions. One test means one signed side/half-plane evaluation at a point, one point–box or ray–box test, one ray–segment/triangle intersection attempt, one raster coverage-coordinate evaluation, or one covered-fragment depth test. Rejected candidates and repeated evaluations count. Polygon clipping tests the closing vertex again, so a pass over m vertices normally evaluates m+1 points.</p><p>“Clip passes” counts calls that clip a polygon or a segment by a half-plane set. Polygon and segment passes are reported separately: a segment is not as expensive as a many-vertex polygon. Polygon passes are individual half-plane passes; a segment pass can test multiple planes. Their signed evaluations already contribute to “Geometric tests”, so adding tests and passes would count work twice. Hover over a test total for its operation-type breakdown.</p><h3>Compare matching scopes</h3><p>Construction animations report the recorded solver operations reached at the current slider stage. Rewinding restores that earlier count; replaying or redrawing does not charge another solve. Accumulated building and terrain shadow modes record preparation, actual overlap and partition tests for each occluder. Receiver-focused viewport traces use those same checkpoint types and can group consecutive blockers making no cut. The selected terrain-shadow mode also progresses receiver by receiver through actual classification, overlap and partition work. A missed shadow performs rejection work but makes no cut. Plane/extrusion drawing is excluded, and counters never interpolate imagined fractional operations.</p><p>Building shadow subtraction processes one building facet against the original bounded scene receivers: ground, roof and wall triangles plus artificial side and ceiling boundaries. Counters include those boundaries; physical area readouts exclude them. Terrain shadow subtraction processes one terrain occluder against all current terrain receivers. A selected projected-cut explanation counts physical frustum clipping for its receiver and every occluder test against the resulting fragments. Its accumulated mode counts physical frustum clipping/filtering for all terrain input triangles plus every processed candidate receiver. The analytical frustum consists of the near plane at 0.01 forward depth and four angular side planes. Display projection and drawing are excluded. The latter's final physical output excludes off-viewport terrain. These scopes have comparable predicate types but different workloads: a physical viewport subset, the full terrain viewshed in all horizontal directions, or a bounded building receiver model. A smaller viewport total alone does not establish a faster full-scene algorithm.</p><p>The point-location demonstration counts the displayed search prefix. Corner events count intervals reached by the sweep; their complete teal polygon remains a reference overlay. A beam path counts processed entries on that path, while accumulation counts every reached branch. The beam target mode counts paths reaching its receiver. Beam and triangle expansion include root containment work; explanatory substeps within one recorded entry can retain the same count. Initial target selection can report zero before receiver clipping begins.</p><p>Geometry/sample pairs count each solver separately. Angular samples include polygon clipping needed to recover physical receiver area from winning camera cells. The planar occupancy raster reports visited grid cells as discrete traversal work; those visits are not renamed geometric tests or ray–wall intersections. Requested hidden surfaces include geometric complement construction. Error checks, auxiliary validation, illustration projection, silhouettes, outlines and optional extrusions are excluded. Surface-patch subdivision, model/mesh/adjacency and bounding-box-tree preparation are also excluded from query counts. Normal construction, area sums, allocations, sorting and other arithmetic are not fully represented by predicate totals.</p><h3>Reuse and timing</h3><p>Cached traces preserve their work checkpoints, so orbiting or zooming the illustration changes no solver count. Changing an observer, geometry or analytical viewport invalidates the corresponding trace. Wall subtraction begins with footprint removal, then adds completed shadows; its preparation readout is a subset of that total. This implementation repeats that preparation on fresh calls, although a production solver could reuse it for a fixed map. Triangle expansion starts with the embedded free-space mesh and index; their creation is not charged to each query. Receiver-first shadow traversal currently repeats shadow-plane construction and is not a fully optimized production renderer.</p><p>The measured comparison times ordinary functions after warmup. Predicate accounting is collected separately, so wrapper overhead is excluded from those timings. Operation types have different costs, and fragment counts alter allocations and clipping effort. Use typed counts to explain workload and matched-output elapsed time to assess speed. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and acronyms</a>.</p>`;
DOCS.shadows.method+=`<h3>Relation to planar wall shadows</h3><p>This tab’s receiver calculation uses geometric shadow subtraction in three dimensions. Each triangular building face defines three angular side planes and a supporting base plane behind that face. Subtract these regions from each ground, roof and wall receiver polygon. The planar wall method uses the same idea with two side lines and one base line, applied to a free-space polygon. The two tabs use different scene models and dimensions; they do not run an identical solver. The silhouette extrusion shown here explains the boundary, while the result is calculated by receiver clipping rather than stencil rendering.</p>`;
DOCS.expansion.method+=`<h3>Relation to subtracting wall shadows</h3><p>For the same planar map and observer, both methods return the same continuous visible region. Wall subtraction repeatedly cuts a candidate region by every wall shadow. Triangle expansion follows mesh adjacency only where a surviving opening remains. A wall stops a branch, and hidden regions need not be visited. Compare their current operation totals below; the useful balance depends on obstacle layout and on whether a triangulation is already available.</p>`;
